---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Track from '../components/Track.astro';
import LiteYouTube from '../components/LiteYouTube.astro';
import { auricle } from '../data/videos.js';
---
<Base
  title="The Story — Panache"
  description="From the 2D Sparklers at Greenock High School through Violent Sabbath, the Slug Band and Auricle to Panache, the Victorian Carriage, a London publisher and a reunion in 2009."
>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <p class="kicker">1968 – 2009</p>
    <h1>The Story</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      It runs from a school in Greenock to a publisher's studio in London, and it
      was written down by the man who played guitar through all of it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap prose">
    <h2>A brief history of bandkind</h2>
    <p>
      Before Panache there was a school, and in the school there were bands. The
      <strong>2D Sparklers</strong> at Greenock High wrote “I Think I'm Going Out
      Of My Head” — Archie Petrie on melodica, with John Dunn and John McGeoch —
      and someone kept the lyric sheet.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then <strong>Violent Sabbath</strong>: Dougie Campbell on guitar and vocals,
      George McClarkin on drums and vocals, <strong>John McGeoch</strong> on lead
      guitar and Alasdair Cameron on bass. Then the <strong>Slug Band</strong> —
      George, John and Alasdair with Raymond Wallace singing.
    </p>
    <p>
      McGeoch left Greenock and became one of the defining guitarists of British
      post-punk, with Magazine, Visage, Siouxsie and the Banshees and PiL. He died
      on 4 March 2004. The bands he played in first are on this page because they
      are where he started, and because two of the men beside him carried on and
      formed Panache.
    </p>

    <h2>Auricle</h2>
    <blockquote>
      From the Greenock Telegraph (6th June 1974), Auricle was the band George,
      Alan and I formed with Ian Parker. We used to play 20 minute-long
      instrumentals at the Burns Howff pub in Glasgow.
      <br /><br />
      Ian wrote some cool tunes and we stretched our chops to play some fancy
      chord charts and time signatures.
      <br /><br />
      Ian is now with the Hollies and has had an impressive musical career to
      date. When Auricle split up, George, Alan and I stayed together in various
      guises, playing the social clubs as a trio called the “Blue Shoes” and
      pursuing original music with other musicians.
      <cite>Dougie Campbell</cite>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      A tape of Auricle survives, recorded on a ghettoblaster at the Burns Howff
      in 1974. They were all around eighteen to twenty years old.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <ul class="tracks" style="grid-template-columns:minmax(0,22rem)">
      <Track video={auricle} />
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap prose">
    <h2>Panache, 1976</h2>
    <p>
      Panache was formed during 1976 when <strong>Alan Mackie</strong> (bass),
      <strong>George McClarkin</strong> (drums) and <strong>Dougie Campbell</strong>
      (guitar) joined forces with <strong>Pat Hamill</strong> (vocals) and
      <strong>Robert Williams</strong> (keyboards), in an attempt to become
      Scotland's “Steely Dan”.
    </p>
    <p>
      They were based in Greenock, with a regular gig at the
      <strong>Victorian Carriage</strong> in Inverkip Street. They played
      <em>Kid Charlemagne</em> and <em>Haitian Divorce</em>, songs by Stevie
      Wonder and Hall and Oates — and all the while they mixed in their own
      songs, and explored tasty arrangements and five-part harmonies.
    </p>
    <p>
      The focus of the band was the unique voice of Pat Hamill: sometimes sweet
      and soulful, sometimes soaring and passionate. The other musicians were
      just as serious about their craft, and Panache became a kind of collective
      music academy in pursuit of musical excellence.
    </p>
    <p>
      There were a couple of line-up changes. Robert left and
      <strong>Alastair Sinclair</strong> joined on keyboards. George left and
      <strong>Derek Taylor</strong> joined on drums. In the few years Panache
      played out across the West of Scotland their sound turned into a funky
      fusion of rock, soul and jazz — good enough to attract interest from the
      music industry in London and the United States. In 1978 they recorded at
      their publisher's studio in London.
    </p>

    <h2>The tapes</h2>
    <p>
      Very little of this was ever pressed. It exists because people kept things:
      a reel from the Victorian Carriage recorded on two microphones into Dougie's
      old Revox, rehearsals at the Red Dragon in Greenock, a jam at what was
      probably Auchmountain Halls, and an Akai ghetto blaster left running in the
      corner of a room in 1977.
    </p>
    <p>
      One of the best recordings only came back in 2025. <strong>Alan Cumming</strong>,
      who roadied for the band, had taped two Glasgow gigs at the Dial Inn and the
      Amphora and kept the cassettes for the best part of fifty years. He ran into
      Alan Mackie by chance and handed them over. Dougie cleaned them up from
      South Africa.
    </p>

    <h2>The reunion, July 2009</h2>
    <p>
      In July 2009 Panache played again in Greenock. Alan, Dougie and George were
      joined by Paul, Suzanne, Brian and Storme; Gerry filmed it and Hugh ran the
      sound; Groovefellas and The Verdict played too. The shows raised
      <strong>£1,100 for CHAS</strong>, the Children's Hospice Association
      Scotland, and <strong>£150 for the Haven project</strong> in Port Glasgow.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      We had a great time and raised some funds for charity. Maybe we'll do this
      again sometime!
      <cite>From the band's website, 2009</cite>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Pat Hamill was not there. He died in the mid-1990s, some fifteen years
      before the band got back on stage, and thirty years before the tapes with
      his voice on them began to go online.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/music/">Hear the recordings →</a></p>
  </section>

  <LiteYouTube />
</Base>
